<o:LogicalDiagram Id="o217">
<a:ObjectID>F804129A-E592-41C5-A44F-C44D91B66C16</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>Lease_Contract</a:Name>
<a:Code>LEASE_CONTRACT</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1679474969</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>Aled.Lewis</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1702991168</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>Aled.Lewis</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>The LEASE_CONTRACT data entity in this diagram contains the base data for the Lease agreement for Real Estate property.
Shell is predominantly the Lessee but can also occasionally be the Lessor, both eventualities are covered by this diagram.

A LEASE_CONTRACT is usually held between 2 Parties:
&gt; The LEGAL_ENTITY data entity shows which Shell unit has participated.
&gt; The PARTNER data entity shows which non-Shell party has participated.
Though at times a LEASE_CONTRACT can contain multiple Partners, eg One Partner supplies the building and another Partner supplies the parking, both of these can be in the same LEASE_CONTRACT.

Contacts can perform ROLEs on behalf of the PARTNER and/or LEGAL_ENTITY:
&gt; A CONTACT can have more than one ROLE.
&gt; A ROLE can be held by more than one CONTACT though only by one CONTACT at a time.
&gt; The CONTACT can be either from Shell (LEGAL_ENTITY) or from the PARTNER.
&gt; A CONTACT is assigned to a ROLE through the LEASE_CONTRACT.

A LEASE_CONTRACT can take two forms: a HeadLease and a SubLease (or UnderLease).
&gt; A LEASE_CONTRACT is a HeadLease if the Lessor is the Owner.
&gt; A LEASE_CONTRACT is a SubLease if the Lessor is the Tenant.
This is illustrated in the model view through the LEASE_CONTRACT self join.

A LEASE_CONTRACT has a time period over which it is valid (TERM), this TERM can be lengthened or ended using OPTIONS.
&gt; A Lease Contract can have multiple Terms (a Contract can have multiple Partners and each Partner within a Contract can have an individual Term).
&gt; A Term can be Terminated multiple ways.
&gt; A Term can be renewed multiple times.

In the LEASE_CONTRACT many details of the lease are established, whether there are IFRS16 requirements (FINANCIAL_REPORTING_STANDARDs), the legally binding conditions (CLAUSEs), how the payments should be grouped for Shall internal reporting (COST_OBJECT) how maintenance is to be managed (OBLIGATIONS), which Taxes are applicable (TAX and WITHHOLDING_TAX) how the costs should be grouped (LEDGER) or which ERP should make the payment.
All this information is written in the LEASE_CONTRACT.</a:Comment>
